[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JavaScript Variables: var, let, and const"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In JavaScript, variables are used to store data values. Understanding the differences between 'var', 'let', and 'const' is crucial for writing clean and maintainable code. Each keyword has distinct scoping rules and mutability characteristics."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"var: The Old Way"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before ES6 (ECMAScript 2015), 'var' was the only way to declare variables in JavaScript. However, it has some quirks that can lead to unexpected behavior."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scope: 'var' variables are function-scoped. This means they are accessible within the function they are declared in, or globally if declared outside any function."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hoisting: 'var' variables are hoisted to the top of their scope, meaning you can use them before they are declared in the code. However, they are initialized with 'undefined' until the line where they are actually assigned a value."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Redeclaration: You can redeclare 'var' variables within the same scope without any errors."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// Example of var
function exampleVar() {
  var x = 10;
  if (true) {
    var x = 20; // Same variable!
    console.log(x); // Output: 20
  }
  console.log(x); // Output: 20
}

exampleVar();"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"let: Block-Scoped Variables"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"'let' was introduced in ES6 to address some of the issues with 'var'. It provides block-scoping, which makes code more predictable."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scope: 'let' variables are block-scoped. This means they are only accessible within the block (e.g., inside an 'if' statement or a 'for' loop) where they are defined."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hoisting: 'let' variables are also hoisted, but they are not initialized. Accessing a 'let' variable before its declaration results in a 'ReferenceError'."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Redeclaration: You cannot redeclare 'let' variables within the same block."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Example of let
function exampleLet() {
  let x = 10;
  if (true) {
    let x = 20; // Different variable!
    console.log(x); // Output: 20
  }
  console.log(x); // Output: 10
}

exampleLet();"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"const: Constants"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"'const' is also block-scoped, similar to 'let', but with an additional constraint: it must be initialized when declared, and its value cannot be reassigned."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scope: 'const' variables are block-scoped, just like 'let'."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hoisting: 'const' variables are hoisted but not initialized, similar to 'let'."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Redeclaration: You cannot redeclare 'const' variables within the same block."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Immutability: 'const' variables cannot be reassigned after initialization. However, if a 'const' variable holds an object or array, the properties or elements of that object or array can still be modified."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// Example of const
function exampleConst() {
  const x = 10;
  // x = 20; // Error: Assignment to constant variable.

  const obj = { name: 'John' };
  obj.name = 'Jane'; // This is allowed!
  console.log(obj.name); // Output: Jane
}

exampleConst();"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern JavaScript development, it's generally recommended to use 'const' by default for variables that should not be reassigned. Use 'let' for variables that need to be reassigned. Avoid using 'var' unless you have a specific reason to do so, as its function-scoping can lead to confusion and bugs."},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding the differences between 'var', 'let', and 'const', you can write more robust and maintainable JavaScript code."}]
